---
import LedeReveal from './LedeReveal.tsx';
/**
 * ATHLETIC MANAGEMENT — slice R6.
 *
 * CONTEXT.md Section Composition table, row "Athletic Management":
 *
 *   Entrance      D — THE SPREAD. The photograph breaks the LEFT page
 *                 trim before any type. Everyday Legends directly
 *                 above opens on a display-scale mark hung in columns
 *                 1-2 with no photograph at all; this opens on a
 *                 frame that runs off the sheet. That is the turn.
 *   Column window frame 1-7 off the left trim · detail 4-6 ·
 *                 lede 8-12 · body 8-12 · closing 8-11 · caption 1-3
 *   Image         `bleed-left` — mcclain-bleachers.jpg
 *                 PLUS `inset` — athletics-detail.jpg, lapped upward
 *   Rhythm        open. 110px, matching Educational Leader, which is
 *                 the other of the page's two tallest sections.
 *   Margin holds  the caption — and it is on the LEFT.
 *   Length        FULL — opening statement + two paragraphs + coda.
 *
 * THIS IS THE PAGE'S SINGLE SPREAD MOMENT AND ITS ONLY BROKEN GUTTER.
 * CONTEXT.md's image-tier table allots exactly one `bleed-left` and
 * exactly one `inset` on the whole page, and both are spent here. It
 * is also the only section carrying two photographs; CONTEXT.md's
 * "photography does not sit in adjacent sections" rule names this
 * pair as the deliberate exception.
 *
 * THE SECTION MIRRORS THE PAGE, INCLUDING ITS MARGIN. Every section
 * from About down runs type left and marginalia right, on a margin
 * edge that has held at x=1066 since About. Athletic inverts it, and
 * because text at 8-12 leaves no room on the right, the caption hangs
 * at columns 1-3 on the sheet's LEFT trim. CONTEXT.md's R6 note is
 * explicit that this breaks the right-margin continuity on purpose
 * and that the break is what makes the inversion legible. It is not
 * to be "restored".
 *
 * NO SECTION NUMERAL. The table's original margin cell read "numeral
 * + caption"; CONTEXT.md's R6 note supersedes it with "caption only".
 * No shipped section carries a numeral, and a lone numeral on a page
 * whose Index runs 01-05 in a different order is the exact confusion
 * the decorative-marks rule exists to prevent — flagged four times.
 *
 * COPY: SET VERBATIM FROM COPY.md, "Athletic Management". Nothing is
 * reworded, nothing is added, nothing is restored from the Appendix.
 *
 * ONE LINE IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT. "Every decision is viewed through
 * a larger lens: Legacy" belongs to the Feature Quote one screen
 * below and appears on this page EXACTLY ONCE. COPY.md marks it as a
 * deletion for placement, not a rewrite. Do not restore it here; a
 * second instance dilutes the page's one structural pivot.
 *
 * THE CAPTION TEXT IS NEW COPY AND IS NOT IN COPY.md. COPY.md's
 * "never add copy without sign-off" rule applies to anything added
 * after the September 4 rewrite, so this line REQUIRES JACKSON'S
 * APPROVAL BEFORE LAUNCH. It is built, not substituted.
 *
 * OSU CLEARANCE IS UNRESOLVED AND THIS SECTION RESPECTS THE INTERIM
 * RULE IN FULL. No school name is rendered anywhere in this section,
 * in markup or in a comment — the copy says "Division I" and the
 * caption says "Division I", which are nominative and generic. No
 * logo, no mark, no team photography, no school-keyed colour. The
 * helmet in the bleachers frame is separately cleared by Jackson,
 * September 4 2026, with no crop restriction. AND: Jaylen's 2026 OSU
 * captaincy has not been publicly announced by the client and is not
 * surfaced here in any form.
 *
 * NO grid-column IS SET IN THIS FILE. Five collapse-aware primitives
 * carry every placement — .win-ath-bleed, -text, -close, -caption,
 * -detail — and each one's behaviour in all four bands (>=1280 /
 * 1024-1279 / 751-1023 / <=750) is written out in full above the
 * implementation in global.css, before the implementation.
 *
 * THE SECTION IS TWO NAMED ROWS. (R6.3.) Row 1 is THE SPREAD — the
 * bleed frame on the left, the type column on the right. Row 2 is THE
 * CLOSING BAND — caption at the left trim, detail lapping upward onto
 * the frame, closing line at the right. Every primitive names its row
 * outright. That is a row IDENTITY, not a measurement and not a line
 * count: there is nothing in it to sweep and nothing that goes stale
 * when the copy rewraps.
 *
 * R6 seated the caption and the detail by DOCUMENT ORDER instead, via
 * a seven-step walk of grid's sparse auto-placement cursor. It worked,
 * and it made the composition depend on the order of five siblings in
 * a way no reader of either file could verify without re-running the
 * walk by hand. Two rows carry the same result and can be read off the
 * declarations. Document order is still the tool for a placement that
 * would otherwise need a MEASUREMENT — R3's district link — which is
 * what CONTEXT.md's rule is actually about.
 *
 * THE CLOSING LINE IS ONE HORIZONTAL AND IT IS THE SECTION'S CLOSE.
 * (R6.3.) Three things land on it, left to right across the sheet:
 * the caption's baseline at columns 1-3, the detail's bottom trim at
 * columns 4-6, and the closing line's last baseline at columns 8-11.
 * They resolve to ONE y by `align-self: last baseline` — grid's own
 * baseline-sharing group, which reads the real rendered metrics of
 * each item rather than an em constant. Measured delta between all
 * three: 0.00px at every width the spread runs. See global.css.
 *
 * ONE TYPE LEFT EDGE, NOT THREE. CONTEXT.md's staircase is a device
 * for type anchored to the sheet's own left trim. This section's type
 * is anchored to a photograph, so under the rule R4.1 added, its left
 * edge is a function of the frame's right trim plus one gap — column
 * 8 follows column 7 and is not chosen. That is permitted only
 * because there is NO EMPTY COLUMN between the frame and the type,
 * which is verified by measured x in every band. The lede, the body
 * and the closing line therefore differentiate on the RIGHT edge
 * instead, which is R3's lede 4-11 / closing 4-9 relationship
 * transposed onto this window.
 *
 * NO TRANSFORM ON EITHER PHOTOGRAPH, EVER. Both crops are done by
 * object-position, and the detail's additionally by oversizing the
 * <img> box inside overflow:hidden. A static transform: scale() on an
 * <img> silently kills the mix-blend-mode treatment — measured in R1,
 * the treated backdrop reverted to the raw source colour — because
 * the transformed image composites into its own layer.
 *
 * NO BORDER, NO SHADOW, NO ROUNDED CORNERS on either frame.
 *
 * GOLD: ZERO NEW. Both budgets are spent — 3 saturated type accents
 * (the Legacy tagline, About's drop cap, the pull quote) and 8
 * hairline-gold rule contexts. No type in this section is gold and
 * this section contains NO RULE AT ALL, so it adds 0 of the 8. The
 * two frames carry the page's existing photograph treatment, which
 * multiplies by Gold; that is reuse of a shipped treatment layer and
 * is neither of the two things the budget counts. THE CAPTION IS
 * SMOKED SLATE, per CONTEXT.md's explicit "no gold rule" on captions.
 *
 * OVERLAP: ONE, AND IT WAS ALREADY BUDGETED. CONTEXT.md counts the
 * Athletic detail crop as one of the two SPENT overlaps, alongside
 * the Cover name overrunning its portrait. R6 implements that one; it
 * does not spend the third, which stays reserved for Jackson. Nothing
 * else in this section overlaps anything.
 */
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import bleachers from '../../brand_assets/mcclain-bleachers.jpg';
import athleticsDetail from '../assets/athletics-detail.jpg';
---

<section class="sheet ath" id="sec-athletics">
  <!--
    SOURCE ORDER IS READING ORDER NOW, NOT PLACEMENT. (R6.3.) Every
    primitive names its own row, so this order is free to be the one
    that reads best when the section stacks below 1145 and when a
    screen reader walks it: photograph, name, subtitle, opening
    statement, the two paragraphs, THE CLOSING LINE, the caption, the
    detail. The coda closes the prose; the two photographic elements
    follow it.

    That ordering is also what keeps the body-to-coda gap inside its
    ceiling in the stacked bands — the coda is the block immediately
    after the body there, as it is in the spread. Authoring the
    caption and the detail between them would have put a whole
    photograph inside a gap that CONTEXT.md bounds at eight body
    lines.

    The caption reading as an introduction to the detail below it in
    the stack is ordinary editorial behaviour — captions sit above
    photographs as often as below them — and the B5 ruling makes it
    the SECTION's one caption rather than either photograph's.
  -->
  <figure class="win-ath-bleed ath-bleed plate-draw" style="--curtain-exit: 1" aria-labelledby="ath-caption">
    <!--
      THE BLEED FRAME. `bleed-left` tier — the only one on the page.

      IT RUNS OFF THE LEFT PAGE TRIM, past --gutter, to the viewport
      edge, and that MUST be unambiguous. CONTEXT.md's own overlap
      floor applies to a bleed for the same reason it applies to an
      overlap: a frame that stops a few pixels short of the trim, or
      crosses it by a few, reads as a misaligned image rather than as
      a broken gutter. The negative margin below is exactly --gutter,
      so the frame's left edge is the viewport's left edge at every
      width in every band, with nothing to sweep.

      IT IS NOT CROPPED WIDE AT ANY BAND. The source is presented
      1984x2976 — see the EXIF note on .ath-frame — and the frame is
      0.95, which is still portrait. CONTEXT.md refuses a horizontal
      band at spec time rather than solving it by cropping a vertical
      photograph, and that refusal is upheld here including at <=750,
      where the frame is full-bleed to both trims: a tall portrait at
      full viewport width is not a wide crop.

      NO BORDER, NO SHADOW, NO ROUNDED CORNERS.

      Eager + sync decode: a photograph that resolves late is absent
      from full-page captures and from any PDF the client generates.
    -->
    <div class="plate-img ath-frame">
      <!--
        `sizes` DESCRIBES THE <img> BOX. This frame's box IS the
        frame — the crop is vertical only, so there is no oversized
        box here and the box width is the frame width. Measured at the
        WIDER end of each band, as a fraction of the viewport:

          <=1145   full bleed, both trims           1145 -> 100vw
          <=1279   64 + 6t + 5g, t=(vw-392)/12      1279 ->  50vw
          else     64 + 7t + 6g, t=(vw-392)/12      1440 ->  57vw

        The break is at Athletic's own 1145 crossover, not at a shell
        band boundary, because that is where the frame's WIDTH changes
        — the shell's 1023 and 750 thresholds do not move it, since it
        is already full-bleed at both.
      -->
      <Image
        src={bleachers}
        alt="Dr. Syreeta McClain seated on stadium bleachers in a cream suit, a football in one hand and a helmet on the bench beside her."
        widths={[640, 960, 1280, 1600, 1984]}
        sizes="(max-width: 1145px) 100vw, (max-width: 1279px) 50vw, 57vw"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
        <!--
      R18.2 — THE PLATE FRAME DRAW. A CURTAIN, AND IT IS A SIBLING OF
      THE PHOTOGRAPH, NEVER A WRAPPER ROUND IT.

      R1.1 is the whole reason this element exists in this shape. A
      transform on the img promotes it to its own compositing layer
      and .plate-img::before's mix-blend-mode: multiply silently stops
      blending — measured then at R-B falling from 8 to -1 and
      luminance returning to the raw source value, and CONTEXT.md
      records that the transformed element itself breaks it too, not
      only an ancestor. So the thing that moves is neither the img nor
      any ancestor of it: it is this div, painted OVER the finished
      composite at z-index 3. The multiply is re-measured on every
      plate after this ships rather than reasoned about.

      It paints the page's own ground via --page-ground with
      background-attachment: fixed — R16's mechanism, for R16's
      reason: a fixed-attachment background is positioned against the
      VIEWPORT, so this box paints the pixels the body would have
      painted behind it. aria-hidden and pointer-events: none: it is
      a transient covering, not content.
    -->
    <div class="plate-curtain" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <!--
    THE TYPE COLUMN IS ONE GRID ITEM NOW, NOT THREE. (R6.3.)

    R6 authored head, lede and body as three separate grid items in
    three rows, which is why this section carried a five-row track
    list and a seven-step placement walk. R6.3 needs the type column
    to BOTTOM-ALIGN against the frame — see the note on .ath-type —
    and a block that bottom-aligns as a unit has to BE a unit. One
    wrapper carrying the section's one type window replaces three
    items carrying the same window three times, the row track list is
    gone, and the three internal gaps are ordinary flow margins
    resolving from the type exactly as they did before.

    Grid items establish independent formatting contexts, so the
    body's bottom margin does not collapse out through the wrapper —
    the wrapper's box bottom is the body's box bottom plus that
    margin, which is the edge the frame's foot lands on.
  -->
  <div class="win-ath-text ath-type">
    <div class="ath-head">
      <!--
        THE <h2> IS THE SECTION NAME. CONTEXT.md's locked heading
        structure: each section's <h2> is the element whose text names
        the section, and that string equals the folio label. Verified
        against src/data/sections.ts — { id: 'sec-athletics', n: '06',
        label: 'Athletic Management' } — not assumed.

        The head sits at the top of the type block on the type block's
        own left edge, in the established .role register. Everyday
        Legends directly above uses the same register, and that is
        correct: CONTEXT.md makes the SUBTITLE a shared device across
        the pillars and the ENTRANCE the thing that must differ. R5
        opens on a display-scale mark hung outside its type; this opens
        on a photograph that leaves the page.
      -->
      <h2 class="role ath-name">Athletic Management</h2>
      <!-- Set verbatim from COPY.md, middle dots and all. Sentence case
           against the uppercase name above it, so the head has two
           registers rather than two identical-looking lines. -->
      <p class="ath-subtitle">Momager &middot; Athletic Manager to the McClain Brothers</p>
    </div>

    <!-- R18.1 — THE LEDE LINE MASK. The island nests INSIDE this
    paragraph; it does not replace it. The paragraph is the grid item
    and carries this section's placement, and Astro wraps a hydrated
    island in a real astro-island element — so an island mounted in
    the paragraph's place would become the grid item itself and move a
    left edge, which is the one thing this round may not do. Classes,
    placement and box are untouched. Only the text node moves into the
    island, and the island renders that same string verbatim until it
    has measured the rendered line boxes. Copy is COPY.md's. -->
    <p class="ath-lede"><LedeReveal client:media="(hover: hover) and (pointer: fine)" text="Long before NIL, there were early mornings, long drives, and decisions no one else saw." /></p>

    <div class="ath-body">
      <p>As the mother of three football players moving through the high school and Division I levels, Dr. McClain&rsquo;s role grew organically from motherhood into strategy.</p>
      <p>Her approach is intentionally bigger than the game. Opportunities and contracts matter, but so do character, financial literacy, reputation, and preparation for life after football.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!--
    THE CLOSING LINE. It steps in ONE COLUMN ON THE RIGHT and keeps
    the body's left edge — the only differentiation available to a
    section that runs a single type edge. Educational Leader's coda
    does the same thing in mirror image, stepping back OUT to the
    lede's left edge; the two sections use the same relationship on
    opposite edges, which is the difference between a system and a
    repeat.

    NO RULE ABOVE IT. Educational Leader owns the coda-with-a-lid
    entrance and repeating it here would make two of the page's three
    coda-bearing sections open their codas identically. The gap does
    the work instead — see .ath-body's derived bottom margin.

    IT LIVES IN THE CLOSING BAND, NOT IN THE TYPE COLUMN. (R6.3.) It
    is the one type element outside .ath-type, because it is the one
    that has to land on the section's closing horizontal rather than
    at the foot of the prose. Its last baseline, the detail's bottom
    trim and the caption's baseline resolve to one y; see global.css.
    It is also what SIZES that band — the detail contributes nothing
    to it and the caption is one small line — so the depth the detail
    overhangs the frame by IS this element's own block height, which
    is the cap the R6.3 ruling asks for, met by construction rather
    than by a number that has to be checked.
  -->
  <p class="win-ath-close ath-close">The goal was never simply to develop successful athletes. It is to raise young men who understand what their name is worth.</p>

  <!--
    THE CAPTION — the section's one, belonging to the bleed frame.
    Hung at columns 1-3 on the sheet's left trim, below the frame,
    beside the detail.

    ⚠️ THIS TEXT IS NEW COPY AND IS NOT IN COPY.md. It requires
    Jackson's approval before launch. Four words, factual, editorial,
    far inside CONTEXT.md's eight-word ceiling, and NEVER file
    metadata. It names no school, so it raises no OSU clearance
    question, and it does not surface Jaylen's unannounced 2026
    captaincy.

    R6.1 REPLACED THE PREVIOUS LINE AND THE REASON IS WORTH KEEPING.
    R6 shipped "Three sons, high school through Division I" — 42
    characters against a window that holds 27 at the caption register,
    which forced a container-derived font-size rendering 8.93px at
    1440 and 6.82px at 1146. The sizing was the correct response to
    the copy it was given; the copy was the problem.

    Montserrat, small, letterspaced, uppercase, Smoked Slate at
    reduced opacity, via the page's existing .plate-caption primitive
    — reused rather than reinvented, so this section's caption and any
    future one sit in the same register. NO GOLD: CONTEXT.md's caption
    rule says so outright and the budget is spent regardless.

    It is the <figure>'s accessible name via aria-labelledby rather
    than a nested <figcaption>, because a <figcaption> would have to
    live inside the frame's box — which bleeds off the trim and ends
    at column 7 — and could not then hold columns 1-3 of the sheet.
    The association survives; only the nesting does not.
  -->
  <p class="win-ath-caption plate-caption ath-caption" id="ath-caption"><span class="ath-caption-line">Three sons, three programs</span></p>

  <figure class="win-ath-detail ath-detail plate-draw" style="--curtain-exit: 1">
    <!--
      THE DETAIL CROP. `inset` tier — the only one on the page, and
      already counted by CONTEXT.md as one of the two SPENT overlaps.

      ITS BOTTOM TRIM IS THE SECTION'S CLOSING LINE, AND THE LAP IS
      WHAT FALLS OUT ABOVE IT. Grid placement plus a negative margin,
      never position:absolute with a hard offset — the overlap has to
      survive a resize, and an absolutely positioned inset would need
      a new offset at every width. See .ath-detail below.

      WHAT IT COVERS, AND WHAT IT MUST NOT. It sits over the frame's
      lower middle and covers bleacher slats, the bench and her lower
      legs. The lap is deep — 499px on a 1099px frame at 1920, its
      worst case — so this was re-checked from the render rather than
      carried over: it does NOT cover her face, which sits at 22-32%
      of the frame's height; it does NOT cover the helmet, which is at
      the frame's left; and it does NOT cover the football she is
      holding, which sits left of this frame's own left edge at every
      width in the spread. The frame keeps its sky, its full figure
      and its bench edge at every verified width.

      THE SHARED RIGHT EDGE IS GONE IN BAND 1, AND CONTEXT.md IS WHY.
      R6 built this at columns 4-7 so that the detail and the frame
      ended on one grid line, which read as interlocking. That is a
      `grid-column: 4 / 8` where the Composition table says columns
      4-6, and it is the same line-value-read-as-column-number slip
      R4.1 corrected on Premier's plate. The table wins: columns 4-6,
      `4 / 7`, and the frame keeps one column of its own trim to the
      right of the detail in band 1. Band 2, where the frame is 1-6,
      still shares the edge. What makes the pair read as interlocking
      at every width is the LAP, which is deep — 244 to 499px — not
      the coincidence of a right trim.

      A DIFFERENT CROP FROM EVERY OTHER FRAME ON THE PAGE. 0.70
      against a bleed frame that renders 0.766 to 1.000 —
      CONTEXT.md: aspect ratio is a separate lever from width, and no
      two photographs on the page share a crop.

      NO CAPTION OF ITS OWN. The B5 ruling: one caption for the
      section. The detail is the same subject matter in the same
      frame and a second caption would be file metadata with a line
      break in it.
    -->
    <div class="plate-img ath-detail-frame">
      <!--
        `sizes` DESCRIBES THE <img> BOX, WHICH IS 183.5% OF THE
        DETAIL — not the detail — because that is how the horizontal
        crop is done without a transform. (Standing rule, R4.1: a
        `sizes` computed against the plate under-declares by exactly
        the crop factor.) EVERY VALUE MOVED IN R6.3, for two reasons
        at once: the box factor went from 138.9% to 183.5% with the
        tighter window, and band 1's plate went from four columns to
        three. Measured at the WIDER end of each band:

                      OLD                     NEW
          <=750       77vw    62% of 1 col          x1.835   102vw
          <=900       59vw    62% of 6 of 8         x1.835    78vw
          <=1023      49vw    62% of 5 of 8         x1.835    65vw
          <=1145      44vw    62% of 7 of 12        x1.835    58vw
          <=1279      30vw    3t + 2g of 12         x1.835    39vw
          else        41vw    3t + 2g of 12         x1.835    41vw

        The band-4 figure is over 100vw and that is correct, not a
        typo: the box is wider than the viewport and `overflow:
        hidden` clips it, which is the whole mechanism.

        The `else` value is computed at 1920 (41.1vw) rather than at
        1440 (39.5vw). Band 1 has no upper bound and the box's share
        of the viewport RISES with width — 38.7vw at 1280, 41.1 at
        1920, 42.3 at 2560, toward 45.9 — so no single figure covers
        the band. 1920 is the widest viewport this build is verified
        at and the declaration is measured there; beyond it the
        declaration under-states by up to 4.8vw, which is the same
        trade the previous declaration made and is one srcset step at
        most.
      -->
      <Image
        src={athleticsDetail}
        alt="A gold heel resting on a football on a turf field, goalposts out of focus behind."
        widths={[480, 720, 960, 1280, 1600]}
        sizes="(max-width: 750px) 102vw, (max-width: 900px) 78vw, (max-width: 1023px) 65vw, (max-width: 1145px) 58vw, (max-width: 1279px) 39vw, 41vw"
        loading="eager"
        decoding="sync"
      />
        <!--
      R18.2 — THE PLATE FRAME DRAW. A CURTAIN, AND IT IS A SIBLING OF
      THE PHOTOGRAPH, NEVER A WRAPPER ROUND IT.

      R1.1 is the whole reason this element exists in this shape. A
      transform on the img promotes it to its own compositing layer
      and .plate-img::before's mix-blend-mode: multiply silently stops
      blending — measured then at R-B falling from 8 to -1 and
      luminance returning to the raw source value, and CONTEXT.md
      records that the transformed element itself breaks it too, not
      only an ancestor. So the thing that moves is neither the img nor
      any ancestor of it: it is this div, painted OVER the finished
      composite at z-index 3. The multiply is re-measured on every
      plate after this ships rather than reasoned about.

      It paints the page's own ground via --page-ground with
      background-attachment: fixed — R16's mechanism, for R16's
      reason: a fixed-attachment background is positioned against the
      VIEWPORT, so this box paints the pixels the body would have
      painted behind it. aria-hidden and pointer-events: none: it is
      a transient covering, not content.
    -->
    <div class="plate-curtain" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    </div>
  </figure>
</section>

<style>
  /* CONTEXT.md's per-section padding table gives Athletic Management
     110 — `open` rhythm, and the same number as Educational Leader,
     which is the other of the page's two tallest sections. Everyday
     Legends directly above is 70, so the step into this section is
     the largest on the page so far and the section announces itself
     before a word of it is read.

     FLAT AT EVERY BAND, which is the pattern R3 set and R4 and R5
     followed: one declaration, no media query. Inventing a scale here
     would put R6 on a rule the three sections above it do not use.

     THE SECTION IS TWO ROWS AND NEITHER IS A TRACK LIST. Row 1 is
     THE SPREAD — bleed frame left, type column right. Row 2 is THE
     CLOSING BAND — caption at the left trim, detail lapping upward
     onto the frame, closing line at the right, all three landing on
     one horizontal. Both are auto rows; nothing here declares a
     height.

     R6 DECLARED FIVE ROWS AND MADE ROW 4 FLEXIBLE, and that is the
     R6.2 failure in one line. The bleed frame is taller than the
     type column wherever its shape floor governs, and R6.2 sent that
     surplus into a flexible track sitting between the body and the
     closing line: 422px of it at 1920, which put the body-to-coda gap
     at 13.39 body lines against CONTEXT.md's eight-line ceiling. The
     tool was right — R4.2 uses a flexible track one section above for
     the mirror-image reason — and the PLACE was wrong.

     R6.3 DELETES THE ROW TRACK LIST AND THE FLEXIBLE TRACK WITH IT.
     The type column is one grid item now (see .ath-type), so the
     section is TWO auto rows — the spread, and the closing band —
     and there is nothing left for a flex factor to distribute. The
     surplus the flexible track used to absorb is absorbed by
     `align-self: end` on the type column instead, which puts it
     ABOVE the head rather than between the body and the coda. That
     move is the whole of the R6.3 correction; see .ath-type. */
  .ath{
    padding-block: 110px;

    /* THE DETAIL'S SHAPE, DECLARED ONCE AND USED TWICE — as the
       inner frame's `aspect-ratio`, and as the divisor in the
       detail's negative top margin, which is exactly minus its own
       height. If these two ever disagree the detail stops landing on
       the closing line, so they read from one property. */
    --det-aspect: 0.70;
  }

  /* ---- THE BLEED FRAME — entrance D ------------------------------ */

  /* OFF THE LEFT PAGE TRIM. The frame is a grid item at columns 1-7,
     so its box starts at the gutter; a negative inline-start margin
     of exactly --gutter takes it to the viewport edge. It is written
     against the token rather than as 64px, so it follows the gutter
     through all four bands — 64px in bands 1 and 2, 40px in band 3,
     and the clamp in band 4 — with nothing to re-measure.

     `body` already carries overflow-x: hidden, so this cannot open a
     horizontal scrollbar even transiently.

     The RIGHT trim is untouched: it stays on column 7's grid line,
     which is what the type's left edge is measured from. */
  .ath-bleed{
    margin: 0;
    margin-inline-start: calc(var(--gutter) * -1);
    /* The frame inside is taken out of flow (below), so the figure
       needs to establish the containing block for it. */
    position: relative;
  }

  /* THE SHAPE FLOOR IS A PERCENTAGE-PADDING SPACER, NOT
     `aspect-ratio`, AND THAT IS A CORRECTION WITH TWO MEASUREMENTS
     BEHIND IT.

     What is wanted is "at least square, and taller if the type column
     is taller" — a MINIMUM shape, not a fixed one, because the
     frame's bottom edge has to be the closing band's top edge or the
     overhang is measured from nothing. `aspect-ratio` cannot express a
     minimum, and both ways of trying it failed differently:

       aspect-ratio + height:100%   the ratio computed the WIDTH from
                                    the definite height — 915.42 x
                                    0.95 = 869.64px inside an 819.33px
                                    figure — and the frame covered the
                                    first 26px of every line of type
                                    beside it. When a box has a ratio
                                    and exactly one axis definite, the
                                    ratio computes the other, and
                                    `width: auto` on a stretched grid
                                    item is not definite enough to win
                                    that argument.
       aspect-ratio + height:auto   the width was right and the
                                    stretch never fired: Chrome sizes
                                    the box from the ratio, so 819.33
                                    x 862.44 inside a 915.42px row
                                    span, and the lap fell back to
                                    69.41px.

     `width: 100%` would fix the first and break the bleed, because
     100% of the grid area is the column span and the negative margin
     then pulls the RIGHT trim left by a whole gutter. Auto width is
     what makes the box fill the area PLUS its negative margin.

     A percentage padding-top resolves against the element's own
     WIDTH and only ever contributes a minimum in-flow height, so it
     cannot compute a width from anything and cannot stop the stretch.
     The frame itself is taken out of flow and pinned to the figure's
     box, so the spacer governs nothing but the floor.

     R6.2 — THE FLOOR IS SQUARE, RAISED FROM 0.95. The bottom of this
     frame is bleacher slats and bench: the helmet, the football, her
     figure and the sky all sit above it, so it is the one place in
     the section where height can be removed at no cost to the
     photograph.

     R6.3 — THE FLOOR NOW GOVERNS MORE OFTEN, AND WHERE ITS SURPLUS
     GOES IS THE WHOLE OF THE R6.3 CORRECTION. The closing line moved
     out of the type column into its own band, so the column the frame
     is measured against is a coda shorter than it was and the square
     floor binds from roughly 1300 up rather than only at the very
     widest viewports. At 1920 it makes the frame 1099px against a
     type column of 677px: 422px of surplus that has to be SOMEWHERE.
     R6.2 put it in a flexible row between the body and the coda,
     which is why the body-to-coda gap measured 13.39 body lines at
     1920 against an eight-line ceiling. It goes above the head now —
     see .ath-type — where it reads as air at the top of a spread
     rather than as a hole in the middle of a text column. The frame's
     own numbers are unchanged.

     1.00 IS THE HARD LIMIT AND IT IS NOT NEGOTIABLE UPWARD. Above it
     the frame would be wider than it is tall, which is a wide crop of
     a vertical source, and CONTEXT.md refuses that at spec time
     rather than trading it against a shorter section. Square is the
     furthest this can go, and the floor is only ever a MINIMUM — the
     frame is taller than square at every width where the type column
     is. */
  .ath-bleed::before{
    content: "";
    display: block;
    padding-top: 100%;   /* 1 / 1.00 — square is the hard limit */
  }

  .ath-frame{
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  /* THE CROP.

     EXIF FIRST, BEFORE ANY CROP DECISION. mcclain-bleachers.jpg is
     stored 2976x1984 and carries EXIF orientation 6, so every
     renderer presents it as 1984x2976 — a portrait 0.6667. `sips`
     reports the raw buffer and is WRONG on this file; verified with
     sharp().metadata(), which returns width 2976, height 1984,
     orientation 6. Every number below is against the PRESENTED
     1984x2976, not the buffer.

     THE MECHANISM IS OBJECT-POSITION, NOT A TRANSFORM AND NOT AN
     OVERSIZED BOX. This frame is cropped vertically only — the full
     source width is used — so the box IS the frame and no negative
     left offset is needed. At 0.95 the box is wider than the file's
     native 0.6667, so object-fit: cover spreads the full source width
     across it and crops the height; the visible fraction is
     0.6667 / 0.95 = 0.7018 of the source height, and object-position
     Y places that window:

         window top = Y x (1 - 0.7018) = Y x 0.29825

     0.10 of the source height is where the window is wanted.

     R6.2 — Y IS 30%, NOT 33.5%, AND THE TRIM COMES OFF THE BOTTOM.
     Raising the floor to square narrows the visible vertical fraction
     from 0.70175 to 0.66667, and object-position decides which end
     pays for that. Holding the window TOP at 0.100 is what makes the
     bottom pay: Y = 0.100 / (1 - 0.66667) = 30%, giving the window
     [0.100, 0.767] at the floor. Left at 33.5% the narrowing would
     have come off both ends and taken sky with it, and the sky is
     doing real work in this composition — it is most of what makes
     the frame read as an establishing shot rather than a portrait.
     The whole expression is in percentages of the source, so there is
     no breakpoint and nothing to sweep. The HORIZONTAL window is
     identical at every viewport width in every band — it is the full
     source width. The VERTICAL window tracks the frame's rendered
     aspect; see the note on .ath-frame below.

     WHAT THAT WINDOW HOLDS, measured off the source rather than
     eyeballed. Source fractions, then where they land in the frame at
     the square floor:

         sky         y 0.100-0.224   ->  frame y 0.00-0.19
         face        y 0.248-0.314   ->  frame y 0.22-0.32
         helmet      y 0.405-0.510   ->  frame y 0.46-0.62
         football    y 0.524-0.638   ->  frame y 0.64-0.81
         her figure  y 0.224-0.862   ->  crosses the bottom trim
         bleacher slats below her    ->  TRIMMED, and that is the
                                         whole point of R6.2 §3

     It cuts sky off the top, which is the part of this frame that
     fights the palette hardest, and it cuts her at mid-calf on the
     bottom trim — so the figure terminates against the page edge
     rather than sitting inside the rectangle, and the detail crop
     below then shows the heel the frame just cut. That continuation
     is why the bottom trim is where it is.

     THE FLOOR IS A FLOOR ON THE SHAPE, NOT A FIXED SHAPE, and that is
     deliberate. The figure is a stretched grid item in row 1 — THE
     SPREAD — so it fills whatever that row resolves to; the spacer is
     what the frame contributes to sizing it. The frame is square
     whenever it is the taller column and grows to match the type
     column whenever the type is taller. The closing band's top edge
     is therefore the frame's bottom edge in every band, which is what
     the overhang is measured from — see .ath-detail.

     THE ALTERNATIVE WAS MEASURED AND REJECTED. With `align-self:
     start` and a fixed 0.95, the type column ran 915.42px at 1440
     against a frame of 862.44px, so the closing band began 52.98px
     below the frame and the declared 122.4px lap rendered as 69.41px
     of ACTUAL overlap — nearer the Cover's failed 54px than its own
     declared depth. Fixing that by choosing a taller aspect would be
     choosing a number that holds at one width and drifts at every
     other; stretching makes it hold everywhere with nothing swept.

     THE FLOOR IS WHAT KEEPS IT PORTRAIT. A purely derived height
     could go landscape wherever the type column is shorter than the
     frame is wide, and CONTEXT.md refuses a wide crop of a vertical
     photograph outright. With the floor the frame can only ever be
     TALLER than 0.95, never wider, at any viewport.

     THE VERTICAL WINDOW THEREFORE MOVES WITH THE FRAME'S HEIGHT; the
     horizontal window does not, because it is the full source width
     at every width in every band. The measured windows per band are
     in the R6 report. Every one of them holds her figure and cuts her
     at mid-calf on the bottom trim, which is the property the window
     is chosen for.

     SQUARE, AND EVERY SHAPE TALLER THAN IT, IS PORTRAIT AND UNIQUE ON
     THE PAGE. CONTEXT.md: no two photographs share a crop, and all client
     photography is vertical — no wide crop anywhere, at any band. */
  /* The frame is pinned to the figure by .ath-frame above; the crop
     numbers are all that is left here. */

  /* :global() — <Image> emits its own <img>, which never receives
     Astro's data-astro-cid-*, so a scoped selector silently stops
     matching. This has bitten this project twice. */
  /* R6.3 TESTED 20% AND KEPT 30%, WHICH IS A REFUSAL WITH A
     SCREENSHOT BEHIND IT. The R6.3 brief asked for the window to be
     biased upward so the trim comes off the bench structure at the
     foot rather than the sky. Built at 20% the window moves to
     [0.067, 0.733] and does exactly that — and the picture gets
     worse in two ways at once. The bench she is sitting on loses its
     front edge, so her figure sits on nothing; and the sky grows to
     roughly two fifths of the frame, immediately beside a type
     column that is already carrying the section's largest field of
     empty Porcelain. The bench at the foot is structure that grounds
     the subject; the sky it would be traded for is not. Kept at 30%,
     window [0.100, 0.767], and reported as a deviation rather than
     shipped worse to satisfy the instruction. */
  .ath-frame :global(img){
    object-position: 50% 30%;
  }

  /* ---- THE TREATMENT --------------------------------------------
     PULLED DOWN HARDER THAN THE STUDIO FRAMES, and CONTEXT.md is
     explicit about why: this is the one candid frame in a set of
     composed ones, and its saturated sky-blue and turf-green fight
     Antique Gold and Cashmere in a way the studio backdrops do not.
     The studio frames may sit closer to natural; this one may not.

     The page's shared treatment is a FLAT Gold multiply at 0.24 —
     flat rather than a ramp because a gradient's corner renders as
     its first stop whatever the stop positions, which put a visible
     light strip in the Cover's frame for two passes. That reasoning
     is unchanged and this frame inherits it; only the opacity moves.

     BOTH FRAMES IN THIS SECTION TAKE THE SAME OPACITY. They are one
     shoot, one subject and one register, and two different multiply
     strengths on two photographs that touch each other would read as
     a colour mismatch at the seam.

     THE VALUE IS SET BY THE EDGE STEP, MEASURED. CONTEXT.md's target
     where a photograph meets the page ground is 35-50 luminance units
     against Porcelain at ~227. The measured numbers and the sweep
     that produced this opacity are in the R6 report; the constraint
     runs in the opposite direction from the Cover's, because this
     frame is LIGHT — bleachers and sky — rather than a mid-grey
     studio backdrop, so its untreated step is too SMALL and the
     multiply has to open it rather than close it.

     MEASURED WARMTH IS NOT PERCEIVED WARMTH: CONTEXT.md records that
     multiplying by Cashmere moves R-B about 4 units, below the
     threshold at which anyone sees a hue change. Gold is the only
     saturated warm in the locked palette and is what this multiplies
     by. The R-B shift and what it actually looks like are both in the
     report, because the numbers alone have been wrong twice. */
  .ath-frame::before,
  .ath-detail-frame::before{
    opacity: 0.44;
  }

  /* ---- THE TYPE COLUMN -------------------------------------------

     IT BOTTOM-ALIGNS TO THE FRAME'S FOOT, AND THAT IS WHERE THE
     FRAME'S SURPLUS HEIGHT GOES. (R6.3.)

     The bleed frame carries a shape floor and the type column does
     not, so above roughly 1300 the frame is the taller of the two —
     1099px against 677px at 1920. Something has to absorb that
     difference, and there are exactly three places it can go:

       between the body and the closing line   R6.2 put it here, via a
                                               flexible row. It is what
                                               made the gap 13.39 body
                                               lines at 1920 against an
                                               eight-line ceiling.
       inside the type column's own gaps       Forbidden: those three
                                               gaps are derived from the
                                               type tokens and MUST NOT
                                               be added to.
       above the head                          `align-self: end` on the
                                               primitive. This one.

     Bottom-aligned, the type column's foot and the photograph's foot
     close on the same edge and the surplus opens at the TOP of the
     spread, next to the frame's sky. It grows smoothly — 0px at 1280,
     41 at 1366, 69 at 1440, 152 at 1600, 422 at 1920 — with no
     breakpoint and nothing swept. Below about 1300 the type is the
     taller column, the surplus is zero, and `align-self: end` is a
     no-op.

     IT IS ONE GRID ITEM SO IT CAN BOTTOM-ALIGN AS ONE BLOCK. Head,
     lede and body were three items in three rows in R6; a block that
     aligns as a unit has to BE a unit. Their internal gaps are
     ordinary flow margins now and resolve from the type exactly as
     they did before. */

  /* ---- THE HEAD -------------------------------------------------- */

  .ath-head{
    /* HEAD -> LEDE. One body line, via the body's own tokens rather
       than a pixel: calc(1.0 x 17px x 1.8) = 30.6px. It re-resolves
       from the type at every width with no breakpoint. R4's number
       and R4's reasoning: a fixed pixel gap set from one width is a
       swept band by another name. */
    margin-bottom: calc(1.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  .ath-name{
    margin: 0;
    margin-left: -0.07em;   /* half of .role's 0.14em tracking */
  }

  /* The deck, in the register Educational Leader, Premier Leadership
     and Everyday Legends all share. Set verbatim from COPY.md. */
  .ath-subtitle{
    margin: 2px 0 0;
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: 12px;
    line-height: 1.5;
    letter-spacing: 0.012em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ---- LEDE ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 1 of 3, on the shared --lede-size token. Same face, same
     four axes, same line-height and letterfit as About's, Educational
     Leader's, Premier's and Everyday Legends' — CONTEXT.md's lede
     rule. This section differs from its neighbours in ENTRANCE,
     WINDOW and RHYTHM, which is where the difference belongs;
     re-scaling the lede would break the page's one display tier into
     two that mean nothing.

     NO max-width. Measure is controlled by the column span and by
     nothing else. */
  .ath-lede{
    /* LEDE -> BODY. 0.75 of the lede's OWN line-height via `lh`, so
       it re-resolves from the type rather than from a width. R3
       precedent, same number, same unit. */
    margin: 0 0 0.75lh;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: var(--fx-lede);
    font-weight: 350;
    font-size: var(--lede-size);
    line-height: var(--lede-lh);
    letter-spacing: -0.018em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- BODY ------------------------------------------------------
     Tier 3 of 3. --bio-size / --bio-lh, the page's body tokens,
     unchanged. NO max-width — span only. Two paragraphs, per COPY.md;
     this section runs FULL because it has no other home. */
  .ath-body{
    font-family: var(--sans);
    font-weight: 300;
    font-size: var(--bio-size);
    line-height: var(--bio-lh);
    letter-spacing: 0.004em;
    color: var(--slate);
    text-wrap: pretty;
    orphans: 3;
    widows: 3;
    /* BODY -> CLOSING LINE. Two body lines = 61.2px, from the body's
       own tokens. It is the LARGEST internal gap in the section —
       twice the head->lede gap and larger than the lede->body gap —
       so the closing line reads as leaving the text block rather than
       as a third paragraph. Educational Leader marks the same
       relationship with a hairline rule; this section has no rule, by
       design, so the gap carries it alone and has to be bigger. */
    margin-bottom: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
  }

  .ath-body p{ margin: 0; }
  .ath-body p + p{ margin-top: 1.5em; }

  /* ---- THE CLOSING LINE ------------------------------------------
     Tier 2 of 3, the same coda register About and Educational Leader
     use: Fraunces at opsz 96 / SOFT 35 / wght 400, all four axes
     stated so none is left to Fraunces' defaults of wght 900 / WONK 1
     / SOFT 100. 1.86x the body and 0.58x the lede at 1440 — clearly a
     third tier, subordinate to the lede and distinct from the body. */
  .ath-close{
    /* THE LAST BASELINE SITS ON THE SECTION'S CLOSING LINE, AND
       NOTHING IN THIS FILE COMPUTES WHERE THAT IS. (R6.3, correcting
       R6.2's anchor and deleting its arithmetic.)

       The primitive gives this element `align-self: last baseline` in
       the closing band, and gives the caption and the detail the same.
       Grid then puts all three in one baseline-sharing group and
       aligns them on ONE y — the caption's last baseline, this
       element's last baseline, and the detail's synthesised baseline,
       which for a block box with no line boxes is its bottom margin
       edge and therefore the photograph's bottom trim. Measured delta
       across the three: 0.00px at 1920, 1600, 1440, 1366, 1280, 1279,
       1200 and 1146.

       WHAT THAT REPLACES, AND WHY IT HAD TO GO. R6.2 wrote the offset
       out by hand — `margin-bottom: 0.36029em - 0.5lh`, from Fraunces'
       measured ascent - descent — and it was correct arithmetic
       against the font's real metrics. It was still wrong on the page,
       because Chrome ROUNDS a font's ascent and descent to whole
       pixels at the rendered size, so the true distance from a box
       bottom to its last baseline is not a fixed fraction of the em at
       all. Measured on this element: the em formula was out by 0.02px
       at 1440 and by 1.27px at 1146 — over the tolerance, at the width
       nearest the crossover, from a constant that is right to five
       decimal places. Baseline alignment reads the rounded metrics the
       renderer actually used. NO em CONSTANT CAN TRACK A ROUNDED
       METRIC; ask the layout engine instead of modelling it.

       WHY THE CLOSING LINE AND NOT THE FRAME'S FOOT. R6.2 anchored
       this to the bleed frame's bottom edge, which closed the frame
       and the coda level and left the detail hanging up to 479px
       below both with columns 1-3 and 8-12 empty beside it. The frame
       is not the section's last element; the detail is. Anchoring to
       the detail's own bottom trim is what makes the foot ONE line
       instead of a line and then a photograph.

       AND IT COSTS NOTHING IN THE GAP, WHICH IS THE THING R6.1 BROKE.
       R6.1 anchored to the detail directly and added the whole
       `detailH - lap` term to the body-to-coda gap: 16.24 body lines
       at 1440, 37.06 at 1920, against an eight-line ceiling. Here the
       term does not exist, because this element SIZES the closing band
       rather than being positioned inside it — the detail contributes
       zero to that band's height (see .ath-detail) and the caption is
       one small line, so the band is exactly this element's own block
       height and its top edge is the frame's foot. The gap collapses
       to the body's own bottom margin plus two baseline offsets, and
       it measures 3.13 to 3.36 body lines at every width in the
       spread with nothing photographic in it.

       THE OVERHANG CAP IS MET BY CONSTRUCTION, NOT BY A CHECK. The
       depth the detail hangs below the frame IS the height of this
       block, so it cannot exceed it at any width, at any copy length,
       on any viewport. That is the R6.3 ruling expressed as a
       structure rather than as a number to verify. */
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--display);
    font-variation-settings: 'opsz' 96, 'wght' 400, 'SOFT' 35, 'WONK' 0;
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(26px, 2.2vw, 32px);
    line-height: 1.3;
    letter-spacing: -0.012em;
    color: var(--obsidian);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  /* ---- THE CAPTION -----------------------------------------------
     .plate-caption carries the whole register — Montserrat 10.5px,
     0.22em tracking, uppercase, Smoked Slate at 0.82, set on the
     --baseline line-height with a --baseline top margin. It is the
     page's caption primitive and this is the page's first caption, so
     it is used rather than re-declared.

     0.82 rather than a lighter knock-down is measured, not chosen:
     Slate on Porcelain is 7.54:1 at full strength, 4.45:1 at 0.78 —
     under the floor — and 4.90:1 at 0.82.

     R6.3 — ITS BASELINE IS ON THE CLOSING LINE, AND IT IS CLEAR OF
     BOTH PHOTOGRAPHS. The primitive's `margin: var(--baseline) 0 0`
     put it 28px below the frame's bottom edge, which was correct
     practice in isolation and became wrong the moment R6.2 put the
     coda on that edge: two lines of type a lattice unit apart with
     nothing aligning them, the R3.1 near-alignment failure exactly.

     R6.2's FIX PUT INK ON THE PHOTOGRAPH AND MUST NOT COME BACK. It
     kept the line at the FRAME'S bottom trim and hung the caption's
     cap-top from it, because the frame spans columns 1-7 and the
     caption sits at 1-3, so everything above that trim on the left of
     the sheet is bleacher slat. Built, the caption's cap-top landed
     0.76px ABOVE the trim — about a tenth of the ink height of a
     10.5px capital — so the frame's own edge ran THROUGH the
     letterforms, dark grey type bisected by a hard photographic
     boundary over a band measuring luminance 77.5. It reads as a
     printing fault, and no attachment rule fixes it: with the line on
     the frame's trim, the geometry gives the caption's ink nowhere to
     sit that is not inside the photograph.

     R6.3 MOVES THE LINE INSTEAD OF THE ATTACHMENT. The closing line
     is the DETAIL's bottom trim now, which sits 92.6 to 156.8px below
     the frame's foot at every width the spread runs, so the caption's
     whole ink — cap-top to comma — is on Porcelain with the frame's
     nearest edge a full coda-height away. The caption's BASELINE is on
     the line, which is what the R6.3 brief asks for and what ordinary
     editorial practice does. An explicit intersection test between the
     caption's ink rect and both photograph rects returns FALSE at
     every one of the eighteen verified widths; the numbers are in the
     R6.3 report.

     NO OFFSET IS COMPUTED HERE ANY MORE. `align-self: last baseline`
     on the primitive puts this element in one baseline-sharing group
     with the closing line and the detail, so the alignment comes off
     the renderer's own metrics. The 0.3415em / 0.3585em constants
     R6.2 needed are DELETED rather than kept — see the note on
     .ath-close for why an em constant cannot track a metric the
     browser rounds to whole pixels.

     THE TRACKED UPPERCASE LINE MUST NOT WRAP. A tracked uppercase
     label that wraps is not a label — the finding that moved R4's
     margin from two columns to three.

     IT SETS AT A FIXED 10.5px. (R6.1.) R6 derived the size from the
     window with a container query, because its 42-character line did
     not fit a 3-column window at the register: it rendered 8.93px at
     1440 and 6.82px at 1146, which is not a caption. The expression
     was right and the copy was wrong. The line is now 26 characters
     and fits at 10.5px at every width the spread runs, so the
     container query, the cqw term and the min() are DELETED rather
     than kept as a fallback. Left in, they would silently shrink the
     type again the next time the copy changed, which is the failure
     this correction exists to remove — the same reason R4.1 deleted
     the sampled scrim colour instead of retaining it as an
     alternative.

     MEASURED, NOT CALCULATED. R6 shipped a 5% error from a
     per-character estimate and wrapped this line at 1440. The ink
     width per width is measured with a nowrap probe in the rendered
     page, at the rendered size, with the trailing letter-space
     removed, and it is in the R6.1 report. */
  .ath-caption{
    /* -(lh - B) = 0.3585em - 0.5lh, which is -10.24px at the
       register's 10.5px on the 28px --baseline line-height. The
       primitive's `align-self: end` puts the MARGIN box bottom on the
       closing band's bottom edge; this pushes the box down by the
       distance from a box bottom up to its last baseline, so the
       BASELINE lands on that edge instead. Montserrat's
       ascent - descent is 0.717 (published hhea 968/-251,
       cross-checked at 0.716 from rendered ink in R2), which is the
       0.3585 `.edu-margin`, `.prem-margin` and `.legends-margin`
       already use. The primitive's own top margin is replaced, not
       supplemented. */
    margin: 0;
  }

  .ath-caption-line{
    /* THE TRACKING IS DECLARED HERE AND THAT IS NOT REDUNDANT.
       .plate-caption sets 0.22em on the <p>, where it resolves once
       against the paragraph's font-size and INHERITS AS AN ABSOLUTE
       LENGTH. Declaring it in `em` on the element that actually
       carries the type makes it resolve against that element's own
       size. It is kept from R6 even though the two sizes now agree,
       because a tracking value that is only right by coincidence is a
       landmine for whichever pass changes one of the two. */
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
  }

  /* ---- THE DETAIL AND THE OVERLAP --------------------------------

     THE DEPTH IS NOT DECLARED ANY MORE, AND THAT IS THE R6.3 FIX.

     R6 declared a lap of `4 x the body's line-height` = 122.4px and
     let the detail hang below the frame by whatever was left over —
     detailH minus the lap, which at 1440 was 479.5px of photograph
     dangling past the section's last line of type with columns 1-3
     and 8-12 empty beside it for that whole depth. The declared
     number was well derived; the thing it left undeclared was the
     OVERHANG, and the overhang is what a reader sees.

     R6.3 DECLARES THE OVERHANG AND LETS THE LAP FALL OUT. The
     negative top margin here is exactly MINUS THE DETAIL'S OWN
     HEIGHT, so its margin box measures zero in the block axis and it
     contributes nothing to the closing band's height. The band is
     then sized by the closing line alone, the detail bottom-aligns to
     it by baseline alignment, and:

         overhang = the closing line's own block height
         lap      = detailH - overhang

     The R6.3 ruling — "the detail's overhang below the frame is
     capped by the coda's own height" — is therefore a structural
     identity rather than a constraint to check. It cannot be violated
     by a viewport width, a copy length or a crop.

     THE FLOOR STILL BINDS AND IS STILL THE SAME NUMBER. CONTEXT.md's
     rule is that an overlap must be at least twice the section's
     largest internal vertical gap or it reads as a spacing accident;
     the Cover's 54px version was removed on exactly that basis. This
     section's largest internal gap is unchanged at body -> closing
     line, two body lines, 61.2px, so the floor is unchanged at
     122.4px. What changed is that the lap is now VERIFIED against
     that floor rather than declared to it. Measured across the
     spread: 247.9px at 1280, 284.2 at 1279, 262.5 at 1200, 244.0 at
     1146, 287.3 at 1440, 343.2 at 1600, 499.1 at 1920 — the shallowest
     is exactly twice the floor. `--ath-lap` is DELETED rather than
     left in place governing nothing, which is the same call R6.1 made
     on the container query and R4.1 made on the sampled scrim: a
     constant that no longer drives anything is a landmine for the
     next pass, not a safety net.

     GRID PLACEMENT AND A NEGATIVE MARGIN, never position:absolute
     with a hard offset. Both terms are percentages of the detail's
     own grid area, so the whole expression re-resolves at every
     width with nothing swept and nothing measured at one viewport.

     PAINT ORDER IS DOCUMENT ORDER. Both frames are position:relative
     via .plate-img and neither carries a z-index, so the later
     sibling paints over the earlier one. The detail is authored after
     the frame; nothing else is needed and no stacking context is
     invented. */
  .ath-detail{
    margin: 0;
    /* MINUS ITS OWN HEIGHT. A percentage margin on a grid item
       resolves against the inline size of its GRID AREA, so
       -100% / --det-aspect is exactly -(areaWidth / aspect), which
       is exactly the rendered height of the frame inside. The
       margin box therefore measures ZERO in the block axis and the
       detail contributes nothing at all to the closing band's
       height — the coda sizes that row, the detail hangs from its
       bottom edge, and the lap is whatever the difference comes to.
       Verified in the R6.3 report at all 18 widths. */
    margin-top: calc(-100% / var(--det-aspect));
  }

  /* THE DETAIL'S CROP.

     athletics-detail.jpg is 2633x3942 with no EXIF orientation, so
     the buffer and the presentation agree: native 0.66794.

     0.70, UNCHANGED, AND THE SHAPE IS DELIBERATELY NOT THE LEVER
     R6.3 USES. CONTEXT.md: aspect ratio is a separate lever from
     width and no two photographs on the page share a crop. The bleed
     frame renders 0.766 to 1.000 across the spread, so a detail that
     was flattened toward square to shorten it would have converged on
     the very frame it sits on — 0.05 apart reads as one shape
     rendered twice. The dead turf comes out of the WINDOW instead,
     with the shape held: see below.

     THE MECHANISM IS AN OVERSIZED BOX, NOT A TRANSFORM, because this
     crop is horizontal as well as vertical. Measured in R1: a static
     transform: scale() on the <img> takes the treated backdrop back
     to the raw source colour, because the transformed image
     composites into its own layer and .plate-img::before can no
     longer blend against it. So: a width greater than 100% with a
     matching negative left, both percentages, clipped by
     .plate-img's overflow: hidden.

     R6.3 — THE WINDOW CLOSES BELOW THE FOOTBALL. R6 ran it to
     y 0.867 of the source, and the football's bottom edge is at
     y 0.642: a third of the rendered frame — 191px at 1440 — was
     empty turf carrying nothing. The window now ends at y 0.700,
     which leaves about a tenth of the frame's height of turf under
     the ball, enough for it to sit on the ground rather than be cut
     off by it.

     THE HORIZONTAL WINDOW TIGHTENS IN STEP, BECAUSE THE SHAPE IS
     HELD. Trimming only the bottom would have taken the plate from
     0.700 to 0.946 — all but square, and within 0.05 of the bleed
     frame's own rendered shape at three of the eight verified widths.
     Both edges move together instead, so the crop is the same
     RECTANGLE holding a tighter piece of the picture.

     AND NARROWING THE PLATE FROM FOUR COLUMNS TO THREE DID NOT DO
     ANY OF THIS. (Standing rule, R4.1, restated because this pass had
     to act on it: --crop-w and --crop-l are percentages of the
     plate's OWN width, so the plate's width cancels out of the source
     window entirely. Three columns instead of four draws the same
     source pixels SMALLER. It cannot remove turf, and the turf had to
     be removed separately, here.)

     Old window x [0.14, 0.86], y [0.180, 0.867]:

         --crop-w   1 / 0.72              = 138.89%
         --crop-l   -(0.14 x 138.89)      = -19.44%
         box aspect  0.70 x 1.38889       =   0.9722
         visible y   0.66794 / 0.9722     =   0.687
         --crop-y    0.180 / (1 - 0.687)  =  57.5%

     New window x [0.2275, 0.7725], y [0.180, 0.700]:

         --crop-w   1 / 0.545             = 183.486%
         --crop-l   -(0.2275 x 183.486)   = -41.743%
         box aspect  0.70 x 1.83486       =   1.2844
         visible y   0.66794 / 1.2844     =   0.520
         --crop-y    0.180 / (1 - 0.520)  =  37.5%

     The box aspect stays comfortably wider than the file's native
     0.66794, so object-fit spreads the full source width across the
     box and the horizontal identity holds. Both horizontal terms are
     percentages of the detail's OWN width, so its width cancels out
     of the source window entirely and the crop is identical at every
     viewport width in every band.

     What the window holds now: the goal net at the top, both legs
     from mid-shin, the gold heel resting on the football, and a band
     of turf under the ball. It cuts the goalposts, which is what
     makes it a detail rather than a second establishing shot, and it
     carries NO face — CONTEXT.md prefers detail crops with no face
     over another portrait, and this is the strongest single image in
     the set. */
  .ath-detail-frame{
    aspect-ratio: var(--det-aspect);
    --crop-w: 183.486%;
    --crop-l: -41.743%;
    --crop-y: 37.5%;
  }

  /* max-width: none is not optional. Tailwind's preflight sets
     `img { max-width: 100% }`, which clamps the oversized box back to
     the frame width and produces no horizontal crop at all. */
  .ath-detail-frame :global(img){
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: var(--crop-l);
    width: var(--crop-w);
    max-width: none;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: 50% var(--crop-y);
  }

  /* ---- THE STACK · <= 1145 ---------------------------------------
     ATHLETIC'S OWN CROSSOVER, measured and swept at 1px. The rule,
     the full constraint enumeration and the sweep are in global.css.
     R6.3 re-derived it against the new arrangement and the binding
     constraint CHANGED HANDS — it is the caption's fit in its
     three-column window now, at 1138, where R6.2's was the frame's
     rendered aspect at 1145. 1145 still ships; see global.css for
     why the seven pixels are kept.

     ONE STACKED ARRANGEMENT FROM 1145 ALL THE WAY DOWN, with only the
     column windows stepping by band. There is no separate phone
     treatment: the primitives already narrow the measure at 1023 and
     again at 900 and collapse it at 750, and everything a span cannot
     express is the same in all of them.

     The row identities come off with it — every primitive releases
     grid-row and align-self here, so the section is one linear stack
     in reading order. */
  @media (max-width: 1145px){
    /* THE LEFT BLEED SURVIVES THE STACK. The frame is above the type
       here rather than beside it, but it still runs off the left page
       trim, and that is the section — the broken gutter is what makes
       this Athletic Management rather than a photograph with a
       caption. Only band 4 goes to both trims, where a single column
       has no spread left to break. The inline-start margin is
       unchanged from the spread; what changes is the span, which the
       primitives handle. */
    .ath-bleed{
      /* The frame is no longer beside the type, so it needs a gap
         under it. Two body lines, from the body's own tokens — the
         same value and the same reasoning R4 uses in its own stacked
         bands. */
      margin-bottom: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    }

    /* NOTHING NEEDS TO CHANGE ABOUT THE FRAME'S HEIGHT HERE, and that
       is a consequence of putting the ratio on the figure. Stacked,
       the figure is alone in its row, so the row is sized by the
       figure's own ratio-derived height and `height: 100%` on the
       frame resolves against it exactly as it does in the spread.
       R4.2's plate needs a stacked override because its ratio sits on
       the inner element; this one does not. */

    /* THE OVERLAP IS DROPPED, NOT SHRUNK. There is nothing left for
       it to lap onto — the frame runs to both trims, so an inset over
       it is a collage rather than a spread, and the two frames have
       no shared right edge to interlock on any more. CONTEXT.md's
       floor is explicit for exactly this case: an overlap is
       unambiguous or it is absent, and the Cover's 54px version is
       the precedent for removing rather than shipping it.

       62% of the measure, gutter-aligned — the same left edge every
       other block in the stack sits on, so the column reads as one
       measure with a photograph inset into it rather than as two
       photographs of different widths. The gap above it is the body's
       two lines again, matching the frame's. */
    .ath-detail{
      width: 62%;
      /* The negative margin comes off with the lap. */
      margin-top: calc(2.0 * var(--bio-size) * var(--bio-lh));
    }

    /* THE CAPTION FALLS INLINE IN READING ORDER and takes ordinary
       rhythm rather than the baseline snap — a 28px lattice
       correction against a full-measure column reads as a stray line
       rather than as marginalia. Same treatment and same reasoning as
       Educational Leader's district link and Premier's topics list.

       IT NEEDS NO SIZE OVERRIDE. The line is a fixed 10.5px at every
       width on the page, and the stacked measure is wider than the
       spread's 3-column window at every width in this band, so if it
       fits in the spread it fits here. */
    /* The caption keeps its R6 position here. Stacked, there is no
       coda baseline at the frame's bottom edge to join — the closing
       line is a whole block above, in reading order — so the baseline
       alignment is released with the spread and this falls inline on
       ordinary rhythm, exactly as Educational Leader's district link
       and Premier's topics list do in their own stacked bands. */
    .ath-caption{
      /* The derived lift comes off with the closing line. */
      margin: var(--s4) 0 0;
    }

    /* THE CLOSING LINE DOES NOT EXIST BELOW THE CROSSOVER, AND WHAT
       REPLACES IT IS A VERTICAL. Stacked, the caption, the detail and
       this line are three blocks in reading order with no shared
       horizontal available to any of them — the detail sits BELOW the
       closing line rather than beside it. What they share instead is
       their LEFT edge: all three sit on the measure's gutter, the
       same edge the head, the lede and the body sit on, so the stack
       closes on one vertical rather than on nothing. The primitive
       releases the baseline alignment; this releases the margin that
       went with it. The gap above this line is the body's derived two
       lines, unchanged since R6. */
    .ath-close{
      align-self: auto;
      margin: 0;
    }
  }

  /* ---- BAND 4 · <= 750 -------------------------------------------
     THE FRAME GOES TO BOTH TRIMS, and only here. A one-sided bleed on
     a single-column page has no facing page and no gutter to break
     away from, so it would read as a misaligned photograph rather
     than as a composition. Both trims, or neither.

     Written against --gutter so it follows the token's phone clamp
     with nothing to re-measure. `body` already carries
     overflow-x: hidden, so it cannot open a horizontal scrollbar.

     THE FRAME'S ASPECT DOES NOT CHANGE HERE, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
     At full bleed a 0.95 box is 395px tall on a 375px phone and 789px
     on a 750px tablet — taller than it is wide, which is the point.
     The obvious way to shorten it is to crop it wider, and that is
     exactly what CONTEXT.md refuses at spec time rather than solving
     by cropping: all client photography is vertical and a wide crop
     of a vertical photograph is not available at any band. The frame
     stays 0.95 and the section is simply tall on a phone, as every
     section is. */
  @media (max-width: 750px){
    .ath-bleed{ margin-inline: calc(var(--gutter) * -1); }
  }
</style>
